interview.answer:
The bug is in `src/reservations.mjs`, inside `Reservations.reserve`: each item immediately deducts stock before the remaining items are checked. If a later item fails, earlier deductions are not rolled back, violating rejection atomicity.

Smallest reproduction: one SKU and two positive-integer line items; the supplied contract does not forbid repeated SKUs.
```javascript
const service = createService({A: 1});
// Before: {A: 1}
try {
  service.reserve('r1', [
    {sku: 'A', quantity: 1},
    {sku: 'A', quantity: 1}
  ]);
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.message); // 'insufficient stock'
}
console.log(service.inventory()); // Actual: {A: 0}; required: {A: 1}
```
The first item passes validation and reduces A from 1 to 0. The second passes validation but `Inventory.take` throws because 0 < 1, leaving A at 0. Execution never reaches `this.requests.set`, so request records remain unchanged and `r1` is not recorded; the rejected reservation nevertheless consumed stock. No implementation changes are needed to demonstrate this.

```javascript
export class Reservations {
  constructor(inventory) {
    this.inventory = inventory;
    this.requests = new Map();
  }

  reserve(requestId, items) {
    if (this.requests.has(requestId)) {
      return this.requests.get(requestId);
    }

    const totals = new Map();
    for (const {sku, quantity} of items) {
      if (!Number.isInteger(quantity) || quantity <= 0) {
        throw new Error('invalid quantity');
      }
      totals.set(sku, (totals.get(sku) ?? 0) + quantity);
    }

    for (const [sku, quantity] of totals) {
      if (this.inventory.available(sku) < quantity) {
        throw new Error('insufficient stock');
      }
    }

    const result = {
      requestId,
      items: items.map(item => ({...item})),
      status: 'accepted'
    };

    for (const [sku, quantity] of totals) {
      this.inventory.take(sku, quantity);
    }

    this.requests.set(requestId, result);
    return result;
  }
}
```

Both rejections are atomic with the fix:

- Stock `{A:3}`, items `A:2, A:2`: both quantities pass validation; aggregation produces `A:4`. Stock validation fails because `3 < 4`, throwing `insufficient stock` before any deduction. Inventory remains `{A:3}` and request records remain unchanged.
- Stock `{A:3,B:2}`, items `A:1, B:0`: `A:1` is accumulated locally, then `B:0` fails quantity validation, throwing `invalid quantity`. No stock mutation has occurred; inventory remains `{A:3,B:2}` and request records remain unchanged.

An accepted-request retry returns the exact stored reservation object immediately, without validating the retry payload or deducting stock again. Rejected IDs are not recorded, so they can be retried with a corrected order. With the supplied synchronous Inventory implementation, validating every aggregated total before calling `take` prevents partial deductions.

Complete replacement for `src/reservations.mjs`:

```javascript
export class Reservations {
  constructor(inventory) {
    this.inventory = inventory;
    this.requests = new Map();
  }

  reserve(requestId, items) {
    if (this.requests.has(requestId)) {
      return this.requests.get(requestId);
    }

    const totals = new Map();
    for (const { sku, quantity } of items) {
      if (!Number.isInteger(quantity) || quantity <= 0) {
        throw new Error('invalid quantity');
      }
      totals.set(sku, (totals.get(sku) ?? 0) + quantity);
    }

    for (const [sku, quantity] of totals) {
      if (this.inventory.available(sku) < quantity) {
        throw new Error('insufficient stock');
      }
    }

    const result = {
      requestId,
      items: items.map(item => ({ ...item })),
      status: 'accepted'
    };

    for (const [sku, quantity] of totals) {
      this.inventory.take(sku, quantity);
    }

    this.requests.set(requestId, result);
    return result;
  }
}
```
